        "original_id": "a39c51e2-4179-5b1f-9c39-f7ab754ad764",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Arthur Rimbaud, Lucretius, Horace, Virgil\n- Works: Soleil et chair, De rerum natura, On the Nature of Things\n- Concepts: Romanticism, Latin literature, Epicureanism\n\nFacts:\n- Lucretius's only known work is the didactic philosophical poem \"De rerum natura\".\n- \"De rerum natura\" by Lucretius presents the tenets and philosophy of Epicureanism.\n- \"De rerum natura\" is usually translated into English as \"On the Nature of Things\".\n- Arthur Rimbaud's early style in \"Soleil et chair\" shows the influence of Romanticism.\n- Arthur Rimbaud's early style in \"Soleil et chair\" shows the influence of the Latin writers Horace, Virgil, and Lucretius.\n- \"Soleil et chair\" is nevertheless unmistakably a work by Arthur Rimbaud.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "09aec400-fe04-5356-a12b-46d7d3d203f5",
        "text": "Page: Soleil et chair. Sentence: The work, while being unmistakably Rimbaud, nevertheless exhibits the influence that both Romanticism and Latin writers such as Horace, Virgil, and Lucretius had on his early style.\nPage: Lucretius. Sentence: His only known work is the didactic philosophical poem \"De rerum natura\" about the tenets and philosophy of Epicureanism, and which is usually translated into English as \"On the Nature of Things\".",
